<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Transport policy.

  BarPro's nginx currently terminates plain HTTP on port 80 only (the :443 server block is
  commented out), so a TLS-only client cannot reach it at all. Cleartext is therefore permitted
  at the platform level, and the only checks enforced in code are in
  BarProContract.configurationError: it requires the allowCleartextTransport flag for an
  http:// endpoint, rejects userinfo/fragment, validates only the path (isPathValid),
  requires a non-default printable token, rejects custom body encryption, and requires a
  recipient number — it never validates the host, so the app will speak cleartext to any
  host with a valid path once the flag is on, and the DEFAULT_ENDPOINT_URL build field
  is never read in main source.

  Known cost, accepted deliberately: the shared OTP_WEBHOOK_SECRET and the OTP codes travel in
  the clear. Anyone on the path can capture the token once and then inject OTPs for any driver.
  This cannot be fixed on the device — the server compares a static bearer token.

  The remote-config and self-update channels do NOT rely on this transport being private: both
  carry ECDSA-signed documents, and the APK additionally has its SHA-256 pinned in the signed
  manifest plus the platform's same-signing-certificate rule.

  TO CLOSE THIS GAP: enable TLS on BarPro, publish a signed remote-config document pointing at
  the https:// endpoint (every handset switches with no driver action), then flip
  cleartextTrafficPermitted to "false" below in the next release.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
